for row,title,detail in [(1,'Chintz petticoat in European dress','Large red and blue flowers and broad winding stems spread across a full gathered skirt; a narrower floral border finishes the hem.'),(2,'Chintz jacket in European dress','Smaller densely repeated flowers cover a fitted upper garment with a flared lower edge and three-quarter sleeves; the white neck cloth is a separate element.')]:
 A(f'2:16.{row}',title,'T',{'Field & Border Organization':detail,'Technique':'Captioned chintz in a discussion of Indian water-based dyeing. The photograph does not resolve the complete sequence of mordant, resist and block application.','Function':'European fashionable dress shown on a mannequin; jacket and petticoat are separately indexed components.','Circulation':'The lecture links Indian cottons to European fashion, botanical interests and the ban on calico.'},'chintz kalamkari floral-trade',sig='economic/trade=Imported cottons enter European consumption and regulation.|social=The garment cut shows local fashion transforming traded cloth.|visual/aesthetic=The different scales of floral pattern separate jacket from skirt.',dist='Indian fabric and European garment construction are separate aspects of origin; neither cancels the other.')
A('','Coromandel textile networks and changing uses','D',{'Argument':'Slides 2, 10, 15 and 16 connect centres of production, maritime routes, warehouses and different overseas uses. The same technical capacity can serve a Javanese court and European fashion without identical meanings.','Evidence & Artworks':'The maps, Dutch warehouse, dodot and chintz garments supply complementary geographical and material evidence.','Course Throughline':'Circulation includes artisan skill, commodity exchange, local reuse and changes of social value.'},'connected-histories kalamkari',dist='A trade account limited to Europe omits the explicit Southeast Asian example.',slides='2:2,10,15,16')
